america is facing multiple crises at  home  this is our historic moment of crisis  and challenge  and unity is the path forward  the biden administration will have to  fire fight on many fronts  you submitted your questions on biden's  first 100 days in office  john fasman the economist u.s digital  editor  and charlotte howard new york bureau  chief have answered them  how can buy them tackle the pandemic and  secure economic stimulus  are more than 10 million fewer americans  employed now than before the pandemic of  course  more than 400 000 people have died so  he's got a lot of work to do  and he's trying to think very  ambitiously about how to help  both contain the spread of coved ramp up  vaccinations and get american  workers back to their jobs back to more  normal employment levels get the economy  back going again  so he has a really ambitious coveted  relief bill  that would give 160 billion for vaccines  he wants more  testing more contact tracing in terms of  economic relief he wants to give 1400  to americans boost the child tax credit  temporarily in a way  that would have child poverty there's a  lot that's part of  what president biden wants to do and  it's kind of an opening salvo we'll see  how much support he can get from some  colleagues on the other side of the  aisle  for this scale of help i expect he'll  get it i expect it won't be  the full amount that he wants he's  proposed a 1.9 trillion dollar  package that sounds big enough to be an  opening salvo  the number comes down and he picks off  some republicans they were willing to  send out direct stimulus checks with  with  with donald trump's name on them i would  think they're willing to get some money  into the  into the pockets of americans who who  need it now too  water biden's plans to tackle the  climate crisis  biden has this very ambitious plan as he  laid out  during his campaign that includes  getting the economy to net zero  emissions by 2050 having a clean energy  standard so you have clean electricity  uh by 2035 a huge infusion of investment  into research the types of things that  we might need including  carbon capture and sequestration uh  batteries  hydrogen all kinds of different things  that we  would need in order to achieve that net  zero goal and then he also wants to  to try to do this in a way that creates  millions of jobs for  ordinary americans and i'm just  scratching the surface in terms of  what's included in this climate plan  and the question is how he would be able  to get some of this stuff through  congress what he can do through  executive action clearly he's going to  try to roll back  some of the regulatory steps that donald  trump  made or rather reintroduce regulations  that donald trump tried to roll back and  try to do that through executive action  but there's a lot of stuff that  really requires the cooperation of  congress and i don't think that you are  going to see  necessarily one big comprehensive  climate bill  and rather you'll see climate infused  into federal policy  in all kinds of ways that includes  through all of president biden's  agencies not just  the environmental protection agency and  not just the department of energy but  commerce the department of  transportation  the securities and exchange commission  will biden reform immigration  well on his first day in office as  promised he sent a legislative proposal  to congress that would create a pathway  to citizenship  for for millions of undocumented  immigrants currently in america  he also wants to reinstate uh daca  deferred action for childhood arrivals  that's a policy that protects people who  are brought to america  as young children who grew up here who  they lack proper documentations but who  have known no other country this is  their home  so he did both of those in the first day  now  laying out a pathway to citizenship  which is something that that people have  been chasing for a long time  requires legislative approval the  problem with that is that for as long as  i've been covering washington for as  long as i've been paying attention to  politics  comprehensive immigration reform has  really been the white whale of congress  people have wanted to do it there has  probably been majority support  for any number of proposals generally  that offer some combination of a pathway  to  citizenship for the undocumented with  increased enforcement at the border  that's always been the sort of broad  contour  but the problem is an impassioned  minority has always been able to block  it  i think that there may be some  motivation there after the past four  years  to get something done but we have been  here before  and so he needs legislative buy-in  whether he gets it is unclear  how can biden make progress on race  relations in america  biden spoke about systemic racism on the  campaign trail  often and i think when people hear that  phrase what they pay attention to is the  second word of it is racism  but i would encourage you to think about  the first word as being perhaps the more  important of the two  systemic racism refers to racially  biased policies  that function absent the active malice  of anybody implementing those policies  that is because  american policymaking has been so  steeped in racial bias for so long  that there are inequities that get baked  into the system and you really have to  root them out  and so i think what the biden harris  administration will do  is in every policy that they enact is  just pay attention  to those unintended inequities that are  perpetuated by any policy they propose  so for example nobody would come out and  say as a matter of policy  we have to give covet vaccines to white  people first  but if you decide to give out vaccines  through pharmacies which is i think what  what  what america plans to do what should be  done and there are  too few pharmacies in majority minority  neighborhoods as there are  then what will happen is that white  people end up getting vaccinated at a  greater rate than non-white people  to avoid that happening you need to  actually pay attention and set up  policies to stop that from happening one  of the executive orders  that that that biden put through was to  set up a coveted equity task force to  say  look we know that there are systemic  biases  across multiple systems what do we do to  counteract that what do we do  to actively deliver vaccines ppe  n95 mass other things in a racially  equitable way so i would think you're  going to see a lot of that  involved in the policy making process  across the entire administration  across all the executive departments the  other thing that i think  is is really valuable and it seems  strange  to even have to say it but having a  president who's very  open and clear about condemning racism  and condemning extremism  is is hugely important we had people a  mob  breach the capital it wasn't just any  mob it was a mob  bearing confederate flags and it was a  mob  wearing shirts with anti-semitic slogans  and you did see um these what i would  call extreme factions of american  life burst into public view and burst  into the mainstream in a way that we  hadn't  in a long time in addition to the  systemic racism  that has been part of american culture  for a long time  um so i think that having a president  who fully tries to push those factions  back out to the fringes  rather than embrace them and give them a  platform you know the importance of that  can be overstated  can biden fix income inequality in  america  i think that there's a theme here which  is there's not going to be one  income inequality bill but biden is  going to  take a number of actions whether  it's trying to raise the minimum wage  expanding uh the childhood tax credit  investing in education thinking about  how to use  spending to mitigate climate change to  also promote  jobs whether it's construction jobs or  manufacturing  all of those things i think are part of  this broader effort to think about  how to help those who feel like they've  been left behind  in recent decades and try to narrow the  income gap  you know i'm not sure which of those  will be  successful there are some that clearly  would do a lot i would highlight  the childhood tax credit which would  have childhood poverty it's really  remarkable how effective that is as a  policy tool  income inequality the climate crisis  race relations these are the work of  generations  but the work of governing is moving the  ball  incrementally closer to the goal while  knowing that you probably won't get  there  and so what you're going to see on on on  the inequality front  is someone who who thinks it's a problem  who takes it seriously  and who'll implement policies across the  range of government to try to make  things better and that's what government  really is supposed to do  how can fake news on social media be  addressed while maintaining freedom  of speech i think that that  looking for a way to quell the divisions  and stop  untruths is misgiving because there are  divisions within any society that get  expressed through  whatever form exists to express them in  a healthy democracy  finds a way to to to channel them  productively to not make them make them  destructive  i do think what you'll probably see is a  lot more attention  paid to regulating the big tech  platforms  i think there's a general agreement even  if it's still tacit you will probably  see a general agreement come to the fore  that twitter and facebook are in many  ways media companies  and should abide by the same rule as as  the same rules as as other media  companies um but i think it's a mistake  to think there's some switch we're going  to flip  to make social media you know  completely placid and honest when that's  not what our society is or has ever been  is there any legislation that could help  to unify  americans i mean the short answer to  that question is  no that's not really the job of  legislation right the job of legislation  is  to is to legislators to pass laws and i  think the question of unity is a bit  of a red herring i think what what  people should hope for  is not unity because there are genuine  disagreements about policy about culture  about the right way to live and a  democracy has to  entertain those disagreements and those  disagreements are are perpetual  um and i think that what so i think what  we should look for instead  is way to perhaps turn down the  temperature of partisanship  so it's not quite so rancorous and i  think the way to do that  the way for joe biden to do that is is  just to be  you know competent and boring i think  we've had 12 years of presidents  who had real die-hard fans who people  were really deeply passionate about  i don't get the sense that joe biden  inspires that same level of  love and devotion and i think that's a  good thing i think it would be good  if americans instead of thinking of the  president  as the embodiment of some sort of  national ideal or as a savior  thought of him as you know someone they  hired to do a job for a few years and it  doesn't work out they'll hire someone  else  as john pointed out he is the right  president for this particular  time you know kind of dull but also  sincere  and i think that we'll see how much he  can get done  you know we'll see whether he's able to  forge the type of bipartisan compromise  which  his team claims he can uh but i think  that  in many ways you know given a very very  difficult challenge  he's probably as best positioned as we  could hope a president would be to try  to move things forward  when he gave his speech on the uh  on inauguration day where he spoke about  how the task of trying to bring  americans together  um it's his big task as president and  that his whole soul is in it  you felt that he really meant that i'm  jon fassman  the economist u.s digital editor you can  read our latest coverage of the biden  administration by clicking the link  thank you for watching and please don't  forget to subscribe
 

