eople end up getting vaccinated at a  greater rate than non-white people  to avoid that happening you need to  actually pay attention and set up  policies to stop that from happening one  of the executive orders  that that that biden put through was to  set up a coveted equity task force to  say  look we know that there are systemic  biases  across multiple systems what do we do to  counteract that what do we do  to actively deliver vaccines ppe  n95 mass other things in a racially  equitable way so i would think you're  going to see a lot of that  involved in the policy making process  across the entire administration  across all the executive departments the  other thing that i think  is is really valuable and it seems  strange  to even have to say it but having a  president who's very  open and clear about condemning racism  and condemning extremism  is is hugely important we had people a  mob  breach the capital it wasn't just any  mob it was a mob  bearing confederate flags and it was a  mob  wearing shirts with anti-semitic slogans  and you did see um these what i would  call extreme factions of american  life burst into public view and burst  into the mainstream in a way that we  hadn't  in a long time in addition to the  systemic racism  that has been part of american culture  for a long time  um so i think that having a president  who fully tries to push those factions  back out to the fringes  rather than embrace them and give them a  platform you know the importance of that  can be overstated  can biden fix income inequality in  america  i think that there's a theme here which  is there's not going to be one  income inequality bill but biden is  going to  take a number of actions whether  it's trying to raise the minimum wage  expanding uh the childhood tax credit  investing in education thinking about  how to use  spending to mitigate climate change to  also promote  jobs whether it's construction jobs or  manufacturing  all of those things i think are part of  this broader effort to think about  how to help those who feel like they've  been left behind  in recent decades and try to narrow the  income gap  you know i'm not sure which of those  will be  successful there are some that clearly  would do a lot i would highlight  the childhood tax credit which would  have childhood poverty it's really  remarkable how effective that is as a  policy tool  income inequality the climate crisis  race relations these are the work of  generations  but the work of governing is moving the  ball  incrementally closer to the goal while  knowing that you probably won't get  there  and so what you're going to see on on on  the inequality front  is someone who who thinks it's a problem  who takes it seriously  and who'll implement policies across the  range of government to try to make  things better and that's what government  really is supposed to do  how can fake news on social media be  addressed while maintaining freedom  of speech i think that that  looking for a way to quell the divisions  and stop  untruths is misgiving because there are  divisions within any society that get  expressed through  whatever form exists to express them in  a healthy democracy  finds a way to to to channel them  productively to not make them make them  destructive  i do think what you'll probably see is a  lot more attention  paid to regulating the big tech  platforms  i think there's a general agreement even  if it's still tacit you will probably  see a general agreement come to the fore  that twitter and facebook are in many  ways media companies  and should abide by the same rule as as  the same rules as as other media  companies um but i think it's a mistake  to think there's some switch we're going  to flip  to make social media you know  completely placid and honest when that's  not what our society is or has ever been  is there any legislation that could help  to unify  americans i mean the short answer to  that question is  no that's not really the job of  legislation right the job of legislation  is  to is to legislators to pass laws and i  think the question of unity is a bit  of a red herring i think what what  people should hope for  is not unity because there are genuine  disagreements about policy about culture  about the right way to live and a  democracy has to  entertain those disagreements and those  disagreements are are perpetual  um and i think that what so i think what  we should look for instead  is way to perhaps turn down the  temperature of partisanship  so it's not quite so rancorous and i  think the way to do that  the way for joe biden to do that is is  just to be  you know competent and boring i think  we've had 12 years of presidents  who had real die-hard fans who people  were really deeply passionate about  i don't get the sense that joe biden  inspires that same level of  love and devotion and i think that's a  good thing i think it would be good  if americans instead of thinking of the  president  as the embodiment of some sort of  national ideal or as a savior  thought of him as you know someone they  hired to do a job for a few years and it  doesn't work out they'll hire someone  else  as john pointed out he is the right  president for this particular  time you know kind of dull but also  sincere  and i think that we'll see how much he  can get done  you know we'll see whether he's able to  forge the type of bipartisan compromise  which  his team claims he can uh but i think  that  in many ways you know given a very very  difficult challenge  he's probably as best positioned as we  could hope a president would be to try  to move things forward  when he gave his speech on the uh  on inauguration day where he spoke about  how the task of trying to bring  americans together  um it's his big task as president and  that his whole soul is in it  you felt that he really meant that i'm  jon fassman  the economist u.s digital editor you can  read our latest coverage of the biden  administration by clicking the link  thank you for watching and please don't  forget to subscribe


